fsimg 0.1.0 (unreleased)

Human CLI output uses contextual usage and help, quiet creation/extraction,
readable volume information and plans, compact format tables and pathname-only
listings. Metadata-loss notices count affected fields with bounded verbose
examples. Diagnostics explain relevant paths, OS failures, layout constraints
and retained/uncertain output without internal error tuples. Verification
announces human success only after worker completion and finalization; JSON
completion records and sandbox warning guarantees remain explicit.
Failed commands omit trailing metadata-loss notices. FAT sizing errors identify
oversized source trees without displaying automatic-size or unused-reserve
sentinels as zero-byte requests.

Reading accepts layered disks. The library parses the `-F` stack grammar,
owns the layer registry and its file suffixes, and opens an image as a disk
whose GPT or MBR partition table is detected together with the filesystem
probes, so a boot sector that also resembles a table is an ambiguity the
caller resolves by forcing a slot. GPT requires both header and entry-array
CRC32s, matching copies, the alternate LBA, the usable range and
non-overlapping entries; MBR requires entries within the disk and the 2^32
sectors it can address, without overlap. Partitions are selected
automatically, by slot, by GPT name compared as UTF-16 units, by type name,
GUID or MBR byte, or by byte range, which never reads the table. `fsimg t`,
`x`, `W` and `i` accept `--partition=` and `-o gpt:sector=`; `i` reports the
stack, the table and the selected partition, and still reports the table
when no single partition holds a filesystem.

Reading accepts gzip and standard input. `t`, `x`, `i` and `W` read a
gzip file or `-f -`: concatenated members are decoded with each CRC32 and
length checked, trailing bytes, reserved flags, a bad header CRC16 and file
names or comments over 64 KiB are refused, and zlib's state is charged to
the memory limit. The decoded image, or a stream copied unchanged, goes to
the spool, an unlinked file the coordinator creates in scratch and hands to
the worker; its logical size is reserved against the new `spool` limit
(64 GiB by default, `-l spool=`) before the file grows, and zero blocks stay
holes. gzip alone is detected from its signature; when another probe also
claims the first bytes the input is ambiguous until `-z` or `-F` resolves
it. A separate relay process copies standard input to the worker over a
pipe without reading it, and a terminal on standard input is refused. The
library adds `fsimg_image_open_stream`, the image options' `compression`,
which forces a registered compression by name, and `spool_fd`,
`FSIMG_RESOURCE_SPOOL`, spool statistics and the reported compression and
file length; image options whose size `fsimg_image_options_init` did not
set are refused before `spool_fd` is examined. Compressed bytes read and
decoded bytes produced count as work, for a file and a stream alike, so
`-l work=` bounds a gzip decode as it bounds a container's. Under
Capsicum the spool, like the
raw intermediate, is limited to the rights a file the worker reads and
writes needs.

Creation writes gzip. `c -z`, or `-a` with a `.gz` image name, wraps any
stack, a bare volume, a disk or a container, in one gzip member with a
fixed header (no name, comment or header CRC; OS 3; extra flags by level;
the creation time, or the epoch under `-R`, clamped to the 32-bit field),
written by a gzip encoder that the transcode stacks under the container
encoder, with its deflate state charged to the memory limit.
`-o gzip:level=` sets the level, 1 to 9, which the library validates. The
raw intermediate and the compressed output are both charged to the output
limit, `c -f -` streams the member, `cW` reads it back through the spool
before publishing, and the plan reports gzip, its bound and the zlib
version. With `-z` and `-a` together the name must end in `.gz`. The
library adds the write options' `compression`, one registered
compression's name or empty for none, and the layer options'
`gzip_level`, and wrap, unwrap and compose compress with it too.

Creation writes containers and streams. A stack naming dmg, vhd or simg
emits the disk into a raw intermediate, an unlinked file the coordinator
creates beside the staged image (or in scratch for a stream) and hands to
the worker, then one sequential transcode writes the container: a
flattened UDZO or UDRO DMG with one whole-disk block table, CRC32 checksums
and image variant 2, which hdiutil verifies and attaches with or without a
partition map; a fixed VHD; or a sparse image with an optional CRC32
chunk. Zero chunks become DMG zero runs, VHD holes and DONT_CARE chunks,
and ranges of the raw disk never written are skipped without being read. The `dmg:`,
`vhd:` and `simg:` keys set compression, level, chunk, alignment, block
size, CRC and identifiers; the plan reports the output bound, the
container padding and the zlib version. `c -f -` streams the image to
stdout and refuses a terminal; a failure after the first byte reports
incomplete output. `cW` verifies the container before the table and the
volume and names the failing layer, which the library reports in
`error.path`. `-a` requires a container suffix to name the stack's
container and refuses the reserved `.zst` and `.xz` compressions. The
library adds `fsimg_writer_output` stream sinks and container output, and
`W -d` folds DMG zero runs and DONT_CARE chunks into its CRC32s without
reading them.

Every I/O object reports which of its ranges read as zero, and the
transcode skips reading a chunk inside one. A file fsimg creates empty, the
staged image, the raw intermediate, the spool or an extracted file, keeps a
record of the ranges written through it, at most 1 MiB of charged memory
however large the file, so its never-written ranges stay holes in any
order of writes and are never read back; the host is not asked about holes
in files fsimg wrote. A DMG or sparse image answers from its chunk index,
and wrap and compose answer region by region from their inputs, so a
container's zero runs and a spooled image's holes are composed without a
read or a decode. A read-only input file fsimg did not write is synced once
and answers from the host's hole report, never for the chunk holding its
last byte, and an input found shorter than when it was opened fails with
`FSIMG_ESOURCE_CHANGED`. An application's I/O object may report its zero
ranges through the new optional `next_zero_range` callback, whose answers
the library checks for shape and trusts for contents; zeroing never
consults it. Under Capsicum the spool lacks `CAP_FSYNC`, and the read
job's image input carries it for the one sync.

Creation writes partitioned disks. A stack naming gpt or mbr places the
volume at 1 MiB as the disk's one partition, emitted into a checked slice
of the staged disk, with the tables written beside it and every other byte
zero. The `gpt:` and `mbr:` creation keys set the type, name, identifiers,
active flag, exact disk length and 4096-byte GPT sectors; the library
validates them and derives defaults from the filesystem, including the
QNX4 MBR type 0x4D and its refusal on GPT without an explicit type.
Identifiers are random v4 values from the command line, or under `-R`
derived from the epoch, and the plan reports each. `cW` verifies the table
before the volume and names the layer that failed. The library adds
`fsimg_image_wrap`, `fsimg_image_unwrap` and `fsimg_image_compose`, which
assemble a disk from table bytes, borrowed inputs and zero runs and copy it
once through the output coordinator, refusing an output that aliases an
input, and `fsimg_writer_output`, which takes a file or I/O sink that
`fsimg_sink_init` starts empty.
`--formats` lists the schemes, containers and compressions with their kind,
support and settings.

Reading accepts containers. DMG (stored, zero and zlib chunks, as hdiutil
writes UDRO and UDZO images), fixed VHD and Android sparse images are
detected from the file's prefix and last 512 bytes, or forced in the
stack's container slot, and read as the disk they hold, with any partition
table inside. Each container's tables are validated completely at open,
under checked arithmetic, into a chunk index kept in bounded metadata
storage that must tile the disk exactly; a damaged or unsupported container
fails rather than being read as a raw disk. DMG property lists go through
a bounded streaming XML parser. Decoded chunks live in a four-chunk cache
allocated at open within the memory limit; decoding is charged as work and
capped without a work limit, and inflate must produce exactly the declared
bytes. `W` checks the DMG data fork and master checksums and the VHD
footer, and `W -d` decodes every chunk for the block-table and sparse-image
CRC32s. `i` and its JSON stack record report the container, its chunk
count, encodings and identifier, and `--formats` the encodings each
container reads and the zlib version. The system zlib becomes a required
dependency, found by configure and never vendored. A range given with
`--offset` addresses the container's disk, so the worker, not the
coordinator, checks it.

Resource contexts account for memory and owned descriptors, enforce session
quotas, and report cancellation and structured errors. Bounded positional I/O
supports regular files, callbacks, fixed sinks, and nested slices. Private
platform tests exercise confinement and no-replace regular-file publication.

Metadata streams, fixed-record arrays, and bitsets share a bounded page cache
and spill to private scratch files. Stable external sorting uses bounded merge
heaps and an on-disk run registry. Logical and sampled physical scratch use are
reported separately; session work quotas and cancellation cover metadata work.

Private writer collection supports descriptor-relative source-tree scanning,
zero and provider sources, strict Unicode codecs, and explicit timestamp and
metadata validity contracts. External catalog ordering assigns stable IDs and
parent links, validates sibling collisions, and retains paged result records.
Source reopening validates identity and mount boundaries; one writer-owned
current source is released on completion, failure, or destruction.
Several directory or regular-file roots compose one catalog: a directory that
more than one source supplies merges into one, keeping scanned metadata over a
programmatic directory's and the first root's over later ones; programmatic
directories with no scanned record of their path merge only when their
metadata are identical, and any other repeated path, or discarding metadata a
directory requires, fails naming it.

FAT12, FAT16, and FAT32 readers provide header information, depth-first
enumeration, current-file content and range reads, and complete structural
verification with optional payload read-through. Directory prepasses reject
long-name/short-alias ambiguity. Allocation chains, directory ownership, random
access indexes, and global allocation verification use bounded resources.
Names use pinned CP437 and Unicode simple-uppercase tables; timestamps retain
wall-clock semantics. Reader and writer capabilities are reported independently.

Format detection runs every format's bounded probe over the image prefix and
opens only an unambiguous claim; several claims require an explicit format. A
forced format checks its own identity signature and never falls back to another
decoder. A boot record without a legal FAT BPB, such as an NTFS or MBR sector,
is an unrecognized format rather than a corrupt FAT volume.

FAT12, FAT16, and FAT32 writers provide immutable paged layout plans, automatic
and exact geometry, free-space reserves, deterministic short aliases and
long names, explicit timestamp-loss reporting, and reproducible emission.
Sources copy directly into the destination image with validation around reads.
Known source aliases are rejected before output mutation, including hardlinks
and slices. Private fresh output preserves sparse zero space; reused sinks are
explicitly initialized. Durable file publication never replaces an existing
destination and reports retained staging and cleanup failures.

Safe extraction creates exclusive private trees with descriptor-relative
no-follow operations, logical output quotas, filesystem-aware selections,
optional timestamp restoration, and explicit incomplete-output reporting.
Same-executable workers use bounded framed control, pre-parser confinement
handshakes, fixed descriptor roles, and a supervised stdout/stderr relay.
Linux and FreeBSD image/source profiles have actual-kernel attack and mount
boundary tests; macOS reports its unavailable backend explicitly.

Automatic FAT geometry stays away from type cutovers following Microsoft's
interoperability recommendation; explicit sizes retain legal boundary counts.
FAT directory labels accept the archive bit used by native FreeBSD formatters.
Independent validation includes native FreeBSD FAT tools, mtools and a pinned
fatcat development oracle, with its sector/Unicode limits reported explicitly.

exFAT readers and writers support independent bitmap allocation, chained and
contiguous streams, validated boot regions and entry sets, embedded case
mappings, timestamp bases, and initialized-data zero tails. Full verification
streams allocation ownership through bounded metadata storage. Compressed
Unicode tables and deterministic plans interoperate with exfatprogs and the
Linux kernel. Sparse-volume checks exercise actual 128 TiB files on APFS and
ZFS at a 64 MiB library budget, independently of initialized payload size.

POSIX metadata extends entries with symbolic links and FIFOs and metadata
with change time, owner and permission bits, and volumes with a UUID. Host
scans import links without following them and, with -p, owners and modes.
Planning checks every entry against the format's capability record: a kind the
format cannot store fails, and an omitted field is a counted loss or a failure
when losses are forbidden. Capability records carry entry-kind and field masks
whose bits are the public values, and the encoded label capacity. Safe
extraction fails on kinds it does not create unless -k skips and counts them.

A QNX6 driver reads, verifies and writes classic Power-Safe volumes in both
byte orders at every classic block size. It selects the newer of the two
snapshot headers, validates the allocation bitmap against itself, and checks
every block a tree references for range, allocation and sharing before
exposing it. Entries carry symbolic links, FIFOs, owner, mode and all four
timestamps; names of up to 510 bytes compare byte for byte. Full verification
reconciles block ownership with the bitmap and slot references with link
counts and the inode table. Native QNX 6.5.0 captures, the NFI research image
and the QNX6 partitions of a Porsche PCM 3.1 hard disk verify. Extraction
reports an entry name the host filesystem cannot represent as unsupported.

The QNX6 writer lays the system run and its zero twin out from block 0 and
every directory, file, link and long name in one contiguous run after it,
so allocation is a prefix and two identical serial-1 headers describe the
volume. Tree pointers come from shape arithmetic shared with the reader;
rows in one paged array are the only per-entry state. `-o endian=`, `uuid=`,
`inodes=` and `reserve=` select the byte order, UUID, inode capacity and
reserve; without `-R` a volume receives a random version 4 UUID. Empty
volumes match native `mkqnx6fs` output byte for byte apart from the CRC
and sync time, and QNX 6.5.0 checks, mounts, modifies, syncs and
remounts fsimg volumes cleanly at every block size and in both byte orders. A
provider's symbolic-link target is read once when its source opens, and a
NUL byte in it fails creation.

A QNX4 driver reads the volumes QNX 6.5.0 `dinit` makes, with and without
`.longfilenames`. It derives the geometry from the slice length, checks the
root block, the bitmap's own blocks and padding, `.inodes`, `.longfilenames`
and `lost+found` at open, and checks every extent and extent block a record
names for range, allocation and sharing before exposing it. Entries carry
symbolic links, FIFOs, owner, mode and all four timestamps; names of up to
505 bytes compare byte for byte, and the label of up to 48 bytes is
reported, so the volume information and its wire record hold 48-byte labels.
Every native QNX 6.5.0 capture reads with names, modes, link counts, owners,
sizes, targets and checksums equal to the guest's manifests, and the QNX4
partition of a Porsche PCM 3.1 hard disk lists and reads completely.
Verifying a format without full verification fails as unsupported before
any work.

QNX4 volumes verify fully: every allocated block has exactly one owner,
directories count 2 plus their subdirectories and other objects their
names, every used `.inodes` slot and long-name record is named, and the
free count is reported. A volume whose label is invalid or whose
`lost+found` holds an entry verifies as unsupported. A volume whose
`lost+found` `..` names the root record rather than the root's `.` slot,
which no native volume has, does not open: it is unsupported. Every native
QNX 6.5.0 capture and the PCM 3.1
QNX4 partition verify.

QNX4 volumes are written in the layout QNX 6.5.0 `dinit` makes, with
`.longfilenames`, names of up to 505 bytes, every entry kind, owner, mode,
all four timestamps, a label of up to 48 bytes and an `inodes=` setting.
Every object is one extent and the loader block is zero. QNX 6.5.0 checks,
mounts and remounts fsimg's volumes cleanly from the 43-block minimum
through one, two and three bitmap blocks, a full volume and the 102767805
blocks of the PCM 3.1 navigation partition, modifies every one with free
space cleanly, and fsimg verifies what QNX wrote to them. `fsimg c -L`
accepts QNX4's 48-byte label.

The fsimg command is shaped like tar: modes c (create, cW to verify before
publication), x (extract, x -O for one member's bytes), t (list, recursive by
default), W (verify), i (information) and --formats, with -f naming the image,
-F naming the stack and a dashless bundle in the first argument. Creation takes
several directory or file operands with -C changing directory among them;
each operand keeps its own path in the image and equal directories merge.
Extraction creates one fresh private directory named after the image, or with
-E fills an empty private directory; both refuse destinations carrying a
non-trivial ACL. The long listing shows mode, size and UTC modification time.
Compression, minimization, standard input or output images, writing
containers and several members are recognized and reported as not supported. Strict option parsing, streaming NDJSON, bounded progress and command-wide output accounting share
the supervised worker/relay path. Manuals, public-API examples, pkg-config
metadata and private-prefix installation checks accompany the command and
static/shared library.

Extended qualification covers ten-million-file exFAT planning, wide directories,
a million-fragment payload round trip, fixed-content volumes differing by 1000
times, sustained instrumented fuzzing, and combined CLI memory on macOS, Linux
and FreeBSD. Dedicated targets separate these disk-backed resource checks from
the ordinary suite and report unavailable platform measurements explicitly.
